.TH SYSLOGD-RS 8 "2026-11" "syslogd-rs 0.1.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
syslogd-rs \- memory-safe rsyslog-compatible syslog daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syslogd-rs
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syslogd-rs
is a syslog daemon that reads a documented subset of
.IR rsyslog.conf(5)
(including file includes), receives local system logging and
UDP/TCP/TLS syslog traffic, and routes it with rsyslog rule
semantics. It is designed to be a safe drop-in for
.BR rsyslogd (8)
on Debian/Ubuntu and RHEL-family hosts.
.PP
The daemon takes its local input from systemd's
.B syslog.socket
(a pre-bound unix datagram socket at
.BR /run/systemd/journal/syslog )
when activated by systemd, otherwise binds
.B /dev/log
directly. It never binds the journal socket itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " " config
Read configuration from
.IR config
(default: /etc/rsyslog.conf). File and RainerScript
.IR module()/input()/include()
directives are expanded inline at their position.
.TP
.B \-N1, --check
Strict configuration validation: parse, check every referenced
resource, and exit. Any unsupported construct is reported as
.IR file:line: unsupported construct
and fails with exit code 1. A TLS listener without a configured
certificate and key likewise fails. No daemon is started and no
port is bound.
.TP
.B --compat
Use rsyslog's own permissive parsing: unknown directives and
templates produce warnings and are skipped rather than failing.
.TP
.BI \-o , --dump-merged
Print the include-expanded configuration with
.IR file:line
origin headers and exit.
.TP
.TP
.B \-d
Accepted for rsyslogd parity (debug output).
.TP
.B \-n
Run in the foreground. Always set under systemd supervision.
.TP
.BI \-i pidfile
Write the PID to
.IR pidfile
(mode 0600). Value
.B NONE
disables it. The service unit uses
.B \-i NONE
with
.B "Type=simple"
or the shipped
.B "Type=notify"
unit, which sends
.B READY=1
after all listeners bind.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Graceful shutdown: stop ingress, drain, print metrics summary,
exit 0.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-parse the configuration. On success the internal ruleset is
swapped under the same listeners; on failure the old
configuration keeps running and the error is logged.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Print the metrics table.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 configuration/parse error; 2 bind/listener error
(including
.B tls_cert_missing
on TLS listeners); 3 internal error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/rsyslog.conf
Default configuration file.
.TP
.B /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf
Included configuration files when the main config requests them.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/system/syslogd-rs.service
Service unit installed by the package. Requires
.B syslog.socket
and aliases
.B syslog.service
so existing units work unchanged.
.SH NOTES
TLS (StreamDriver.Mode="1") is fail-closed: no certificate and
key means the listener fails to start; there is no plaintext
fallback on a TLS port. Peer authentication modes:
.B "x509/name"
(chain plus SAN name match against PermittedPeer) and
.B "x509/certvalid"
(chain only). The
.B anon
and
.B fingerprint
and TLS driver modes are parsed using rsyslog-compatible names
but accepted only where safe.
.PP
Not supported (strict parse error when configured): RELP,
imjournal/omjournal, database/shell/pipe actions, disk queues,
full RainerScript (if/call/ruleset), non-* omusrmsg users,
and non-rsyslog (syslog-ng) configuration files. Use
.BR syslogng2rsyslog(1)
for common syslog-ng patterns instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsyslog.conf (5),
.BR syslogng2rsyslog (1),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
